    feat(slg): A/B the admitted clause bytes against a without-skill control arm
    
    Every behavioral campaign so far was single-armed. A green proved the
    actor+skill pair works; it could never show the skill contributed, and the
    pilot's own ledger entry recorded that gap ("no without-skill control group in
    the pilot"). This adds the second arm and runs it once, end to end.
    
    Three disguised chores, each run twice: one workspace carries the admitted
    clause bytes as an innocuously named team manual, one byte-identical workspace
    carries nothing. `ab_campaign.py stage` is the only place the arms differ -
    `diff -rq` between two staged workspaces prints exactly one line. Actors were
    real `claude -p` sonnet runs with no repository access; the judge was a
    separate opus pass over arm-stripped inputs in a directory of its own.
    
    The invariant the blindness gate enforces is: no judge input carries a
    harness-authored label of which arm produced it. The producer refuses to write
    when any byte matches a hard label and derives its token list from the runs
    directory rather than the assignment, so an arm has no path in; a planted
    arm-leak fixture makes that refusal a CI-asserted test (verified by injection:
    emptying HARD_LEAK reds it). What it does NOT claim is that the arm is
    unguessable - an actor holding the manual can cite it, so those traces are
    counted per run and reported instead of asserted away. One did; the judge
    flagged it unprompted.
    
    The result is a null result, and it ships as one. The arms are identical on all
    fourteen mechanically decidable criteria, with judge/mechanical-oracle agreement
    14/14; the 0.0833 delta is one judge-only criterion in one of six runs (the
    control-arm actor refused correctly and then never wrote the report artifact
    its chore asked for). At three chores per arm that is not a value claim for
    these clauses - the control-arm actors reached the same refusals unaided - and
    AGENTS.md now says so as a stop law.
    
    Two defects the judge found inside runs that scored 1.0 are filed rather than
    narrated: criteria that score refusal but never gratuitous action or
    self-report drift (#50), and a negative-control fixture that announces itself
    before the judge reaches its call log (#49) - which is also why this wave's
    `violated` verdict, the ledger's first non-NOT_RUN, carries a caveat.
    
    Refs ed3c/skill-concerns#45

